            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life and Education</strong><br>\nJohn Stuart Mill was born in London on May 20, 1806, to Harriet Burrow and the Scottish philosopher James Mill. His father, a close associate of Jeremy Bentham, gave him a rigorous upbringing: he was educated at home and was deliberately shielded from children his age to foster his intellectual development.</p>\n\n<p><strong>His Career</strong><br>\nMill pursued a career with the East India Company and used his spare time to write and explore philosophical ideas. His employment there from 1823 until the company's dissolution in 1858 allowed him stability while he developed his ideas on liberty, utilitarianism, and political economy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contributions to Utilitarianism</strong><br>\nMill is best known for his work in philosophy and political economy, advocating utilitarianism—the idea that the best action is the one that maximizes utility, usually defined as that which produces the greatest well-being of the greatest number of people.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Feminism and Social Reform</strong><br>\nA forward-thinker, Mill was a proponent of individual freedom and women's rights. His book \"The Subjection of Women\" (1869) argued for equality between the sexes, a radical view for the time. He believed in the power of societal reform and education to improve individual happiness.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"On Liberty\"</strong><br>\nIn \"On Liberty\" (1859), Mill discussed the nature and limits of the power that society can legitimately exercise over the individual. He feared \"tyranny of the majority\" and advocated for personal freedom as long as it did not harm others.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Involvement</strong><br>\nMill served as a Member of Parliament for Westminster from 1865 to 1868. He was an influential figure in British liberalism and was instrumental in various social reforms, including the push for the extension of voting rights beyond the property-owning classes.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
