          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Economic Philosophy</strong><br>\nIn his seminal work \"Principles of Political Economy\" (1848), Mill formulated ideas that contributed to classical economics. He emphasized the importance of intellectual and moral progress in society over mere economic growth.</p>\n\n<p><strong>His Personal Life</strong><br>\nMill married Harriet Taylor in 1851 after a twenty-year friendship. She had a significant influence on his work, and her death in 1858 deeply affected him. His later works are considered by some to reflect a more emotional engagement with social issues, potentially stemming from her influence.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nJohn Stuart Mill died on May 8, 1873, in Avignon, France. His ideas on liberty, utility, and equality continue to influence philosophical and economic thought, underpinning modern discussions on democracy and human rights.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
